In mobile-edge federated learning (FL), full-model updates stop arriving once round deadlines bind on degraded uplinks: the federation does not learn more slowly, it stops learning. Federated low-rank adaptation (LoRA) shrinks the payload, but existing systems set the rank from training-side signals or channel statistics, never from the delivery probability of the specific payload on a lossy, deadline-bound link---although a larger rank buys adaptation capacity and loses exactly that probability. FedRAS treats the rank as a schedulable network resource and decides admission and rank jointly under a byte budget and a participation cap: client--rank pairs are scored by a payload-conditioned delivery hazard and a saturating capacity gain, the assignment is solved exactly on a byte grid with a provably optimal cheap fallback, and heterogeneous ranks are merged by exact frozen-factor averaging. All results are reported in one currency, the adaptation capacity actually delivered on time. Under deadline stress, FedRAS delivers the most measured capacity of the evaluated policies---22\% more timely updates than fixed rank 16 at 23\% fewer bytes, and 42\% more delivered capacity than the timely-count leader fixed rank 4---at statistically equivalent accuracy, while full-model updates miss every stress deadline; a backbone-quality sweep locates where preserved capacity converts into accuracy, up to 1.3 points over link-oblivious schedules; and at 500 clients exact scheduling dominates greedy heuristics at sub-second cost whenever the budget binds.
